<section
  id="methodology"
  class="methodology"
  aria-labelledby="methodology-heading"
>
  <header>
    <h2 id="methodology-heading">How the model works</h2>
    <p>
      The sliders feed a deliberately simple illustrative model. Its weights are
      transparent design choices, not empirical predictions.
    </p>
  </header>

  <details>
    <summary>View methodology and formulas</summary>
    <div class="method-body">
      <section aria-labelledby="sequence-heading">
        <h3 id="sequence-heading">Scoring sequence</h3>
        <ol class="formula-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Institutional capacity</strong>
            <code>
              0.21(ownership) + 0.18(democracy) + 0.16(social dividend) +
              0.13(worker power) + 0.13(civil liberties) + 0.11(universal
              access) + 0.08(open infrastructure)
            </code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Automation amplifier</strong>
            <code>0.55 + 0.90 × (automation / 100)</code>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Outcome position</strong>
            <code
              >clamp(50 + (institutional capacity − 50) × amplifier, 0, 100)</code
            >
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Low automation compresses the range toward the middle. High automation
          makes the selected institutions more consequential.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
        <h3 id="limits-heading">Limitations</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Weights and categories are normative choices.</li>
          <li>Real societies contain feedback loops and path dependence.</li>
          <li>
            The model omits culture, ecology, conflict, and international
            variation.
          </li>
          <li>Formal institutions may work differently in practice.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The aim is to make assumptions visible and discussable. The weights,
          categories, and omissions should be challenged.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="guard-note" aria-labelledby="guard-heading">
        <h3 id="guard-heading">Extreme tail-risk guard</h3>
        <p>
          Eliminationist Regime appears only when automation is at least 85;
          ownership, democracy, and civil liberties are 12 or lower; social
          dividend and worker power are 15 or lower; and the outcome position is
          below 12.
        </p>
        <p>
          Genocide also requires dehumanizing ideology, institutions,
          perpetrators, and destroyed restraints. AI does not produce it on its
          own; extreme automation could remove practical barriers to coercion
          only if those protections failed as well.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="indicator-formulas" aria-labelledby="indicator-heading">
        <h3 id="indicator-heading">Structural indicators</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <code
              >Material abundance = 0.65 automation + 0.35 universal access</code
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <code
              >Shared prosperity = 0.40 ownership + 0.25 social dividend + 0.20
              worker power + 0.15 universal access</code
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <code
              >Personal autonomy = 0.35 democracy + 0.30 civil liberties + 0.20
              worker power + 0.15 universal access</code
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <code
              >Political security = 0.55 democracy + 0.30 civil liberties + 0.15
              ownership</code
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <code
              >Freedom from compulsory work = automation × [0.30 + 0.70 ×
              ((ownership + social dividend + universal access) / 300)]</code
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Every indicator is clamped to 0–100. These are conceptual composites,
          not measurements.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

